	<name>Yale Assure Lock 2 (Wi-Fi Smart Module)</name>
	<model>YRD4xx / YRD5xx with Wi-Fi Smart Module (Yale Access cloud)</model>
	<created>09/03/2026 12:00 PM</created>
	<modified>09/03/2026 07:05 PM</modified>
	<version>117</version>
	<small image_source="c4z">icons/device_sm.png</small>
	<large image_source="c4z">icons/device_lg.png</large>
	<control>lua_gen</control>
	<controlmethod>ip</controlmethod>
	<driver>DriverWorks</driver>
	<templatedescription>Yale Assure Lock 2 with the Wi-Fi Smart Module, controlled through the Yale Access (August) cloud API over HTTPS. Lock/unlock, lock state, battery, DoorSense contact, event history, and read-only keypad PIN listing.</templatedescription>
	<search_types>
		<type>Yale:lock:AssureLock2-WiFi</type>
	</search_types>

	<!--
		NOTE ON CONNECTIONS: this driver has NO TCP/network connection binding.
		The Assure Lock 2 Wi-Fi Smart Module has no LAN API - all control goes
		out to https://api-production.august.com over HTTPS, which the driver
		performs with C4:url(). C4:url() does not use a network binding, so
		declaring a TCP port here would create a binding that never connects and
		would show as a permanently offline network device in Composer.
		<controlmethod> is still "ip" because the device is reached over IP.

		NOTE ON CLASSNAMES - now VERIFIED, no longer a guess. The lock proxy
		reference publishes no example <connections> block, so this was
		originally written from convention. It has since been checked against
		two authoritative sources pulled out of a live Control4 project:
		  * Control4's own lock proxy definition, lock.c4i (Copyright 2016
		    Control4 Corporation), which declares connection id 5001,
		    facing 6, TYPE 2, consumer True, classname LOCK.
		  * Cinegration's shipping yale_lock_cin.c4z, which declares its
		    lock proxy binding as id 5001, facing 6, TYPE 2, consumer False,
		    classname LOCK, hidden True.
		This driver now matches on type 2 (v100 had 1, which was wrong).

		v108 REMOVED <hidden>True</hidden> from this connection. It was copied
		from Cinegration in v101, but their 5001 is an internal link from their
		driver to a SEPARATE lock.c4i proxy DEVICE, and that separate device is
		what Navigator enumerates. Here, 5001 IS this device's own lock proxy
		binding, so hiding it hid the lock from Navigator: under
		Security > Locks & Sensors the room listed only the generic relay
		"Door Lock" and this driver's DoorSense contact - the lock itself
		appeared in neither the Visible nor the Hidden list, i.e. Navigator did
		not consider it a lock at all. Control4's own lock.c4i has no <hidden>.
		Do not re-add it.

		lock.c4i also exposes a second connection (id 1, "Lock State", type 1,
		classname RELAY) so the proxy can drive a Control4 Generic Door Lock.
		That binding is provided by the PROXY itself, not by the driver, so
		there is deliberately nothing to declare for it here - it shows up on
		the proxy automatically once this driver is placed.

		CONTACT_SENSOR and <type>1</type> for the DoorSense binding come from
		the contact proxy reference, which does document its connections block.
	-->
	<!--
		v117: the DoorSense contact is NO LONGER a proxy this driver hosts.

		It was declared as a second hosted proxy with connection 5002 as
		type 1 / consumer TRUE. That is the shape of a CONSUMER control input - a
		binding that accepts a contact sensor from some other device - so Composer
		listed "Door Position (DoorSense)" under Control Inputs, and nothing this
		driver sent ever reached the contact device. It sat at its default state
		(Open) permanently. That is why the reading did not change across every
		combination of notification polarity and the Invert Sensor checkbox: the
		polarity was never the variable.

		Control4's own drivers surface a contact the other way round, as a
		DEPENDENCY: the driver declares a provider binding (id < 5000,
		consumer False) and a <dependency> naming the contact proxy, and Composer
		auto-adds that proxy as its own device bound to it. Verified against
		control4_doorstation_ds2.c4z, which does exactly this with
		contactsingle_motionsensor_c4.c4i on binding 300.
	-->
	<proxies qty="1">
		<proxy image_source="c4z" small_image="icons/device_sm.png" large_image="icons/device_lg.png" proxybindingid="5001" name="Yale Assure Lock 2">lock</proxy>
	</proxies>

	<dependencies>
		<dependency alwaysAdd="true" addMulitple="false">
			<name>contactsingle_contactswitch_c4.c4i</name>
			<auto_bindings>
				<auto_binding>
					<id>300</id>
					<isconsumer>false</isconsumer>
					<classes>
						<class>
							<classname>CONTACT_SENSOR</classname>
						</class>
					</classes>
					<dep_binding_id>1</dep_binding_id>
				</auto_binding>
			</auto_bindings>
		</dependency>
	</dependencies>

	<connections>
		<connection>
			<id>5001</id>
			<facing>6</facing>
			<connectionname>Lock</connectionname>
			<type>2</type>
			<!--
				consumer False, matching Control4's own drivers: lock_consolidator.c4z
				and door_autolock.c4z both declare their driver's OWN proxy binding as
				type 2 / consumer False. v113 tried True on the theory that it should
				mirror lock.c4i's own declaration; it changed nothing and no working
				example supports it, so it is reverted rather than left in as cargo.
			-->
			<consumer>False</consumer>
			<audiosource>False</audiosource>
			<videosource>False</videosource>
			<linelevel>False</linelevel>
			<classes>
				<class>
					<classname>LOCK</classname>
				</class>
			</classes>
		</connection>
		<!--
			PROVIDER binding for the DoorSense contact. consumer False: this driver
			DRIVES the contact device, it does not read one. Binding ids below 5000
			are the convention for non-proxy bindings; 300 matches the doorstation
			driver's motion-sensor binding, which is the reference for this pattern.
		-->
		<connection>
			<id>300</id>
			<facing>6</facing>
			<connectionname>Door Position (DoorSense)</connectionname>
			<type>1</type>
			<consumer>False</consumer>
			<audiosource>False</audiosource>
			<videosource>False</videosource>
			<linelevel>False</linelevel>
			<classes>
				<class>
					<classname>CONTACT_SENSOR</classname>
				</class>
			</classes>
		</connection>
	</connections>

	<!--
		Capabilities are declared here AND re-sent at runtime as
		CAPABILITY_CHANGED notifications (see PublishCapabilities() in
		driver.lua), so the values survive a proxy that reads them either way.

		Everything settings-related is false on purpose: the Yale Access API has
		no confirmed endpoints for auto-lock time, volume, language, lockout
		timers or admin codes, so advertising them would render Navigator
		screens whose controls silently do nothing.

		has_internal_history is true - REQUEST_HISTORY is implemented against
		GET /houses/{houseId}/activities.

		max_users is the Assure Lock 2's published 250-code capacity. NOTE that
		user/PIN WRITES (ADD_USER / EDIT_USER / DELETE_USER) are not implemented
		- no confirmed API endpoint exists for them; only reading the code list
		is supported. See the KNOWN GAP section at the top of driver.lua.
	-->
	<capabilities>
		<!--
			v114: <navigator_display_option> REMOVED from the static capabilities.

			It was added during the icon work to give Navigator custom lock artwork.
			It is also the only thing declared against binding 5001 that is not
			declared against 5002 - and 5002 (the DoorSense contact) is enumerated by
			Director while 5001 (the lock) is not: the room's Navigator tab, under
			Security > Locks & Sensors, lists the contact and offers the lock in
			neither the Visible nor the Hidden column. Same device, same room, one
			proxy listed and one not, so the difference is in what 5001 declares.

			Custom Navigator artwork is cosmetic; a lock that Navigator will not show
			at all is not. If removing this restores the lock, the icons can be
			reintroduced deliberately and verified one at a time.
		-->
		<has_settings>false</has_settings>
		<has_admin_code>false</has_admin_code>
		<has_auto_lock_time>false</has_auto_lock_time>
		<has_custom_settings>false</has_custom_settings>
		<has_daily_schedule>false</has_daily_schedule>
		<has_date_range_schedule>false</has_date_range_schedule>
		<has_internal_history>true</has_internal_history>
		<has_language>false</has_language>
		<has_lock_modes>false</has_lock_modes>
		<has_log_failed_attempts>false</has_log_failed_attempts>
		<has_log_item_count>false</has_log_item_count>
		<has_one_touch_locking>false</has_one_touch_locking>
		<has_schedule_lockout>false</has_schedule_lockout>
		<has_shutout_timer>false</has_shutout_timer>
		<has_volume>false</has_volume>
		<has_wrong_code_attempts>false</has_wrong_code_attempts>
		<is_management_only>false</is_management_only>
		<max_users>250</max_users>

		<!--
			These three keys are NOT in the published lock proxy reference. They
			were found in Cinegration's shipping yale_lock_cin.c4z, which sets all
			three false for this same lock. They are the correct, first-class way
			to tell Navigator that this lock's credential list is read-only, so it
			hides the add/edit/delete controls instead of offering buttons that do
			nothing. This replaces the earlier approach of accepting ADD_USER /
			EDIT_USER / DELETE_USER and silently logging - the driver still guards
			those commands, but the UI should no longer offer them at all.

			Note this is independent confirmation that PIN writes are not merely
			undocumented but genuinely unavailable on this API: a paid commercial
			driver for the same lock also declines to implement them.
		-->
		<can_add_remove_user>false</can_add_remove_user>
		<can_edit_user>false</can_edit_user>
		<can_edit_user_pin>false</can_edit_user_pin>

		<!--
			Files this driver's activity under Security > Door Lock in Control4's
			History. Taken from Control4's own lock.c4i, which declares exactly
			these two keys.
		-->
		<history_category>Security</history_category>
		<history_subcategory>Door Lock</history_subcategory>
	</capabilities>

	<config>
		<power_management_method>AlwaysOn</power_management_method>
		<power_command_delay>0</power_command_delay>
		<power_delay>0</power_delay>
		<power_command_needed>False</power_command_needed>
		<documentation file="www\documentation.rtf" />
		<script file="driver.lua" />

		<actions>
			<action>
				<name>1 - Sign In / Request Verification Code</name>
				<command>ACTION_SIGN_IN</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name>2 - Submit Verification Code</name>
				<command>ACTION_SUBMIT_CODE</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name>3 - Discover Locks</name>
				<command>ACTION_DISCOVER</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name> - Refresh Status</name>
				<command>ACTION_REFRESH_STATUS</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name> - Refresh History</name>
				<command>ACTION_REFRESH_HISTORY</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name> - Refresh PIN Codes</name>
				<command>ACTION_REFRESH_USERS</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name> - Refresh Access Token</name>
				<command>ACTION_REFRESH_TOKEN</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name> - Clear Stored Credentials</name>
				<command>ACTION_CLEAR_CREDENTIALS</command>
			</action>
			<action>
				<name> - Print Debug Info</name>
				<command>ACTION_PRINT_DEBUG</command>
			</action>
		</actions>

		<properties>
			<property>
				<name>Brand</name>
				<type>LIST</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default>Yale Access</default>
				<items>
					<item>Yale Access</item>
					<item>August</item>
				</items>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Login Method</name>
				<type>LIST</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default>Email</default>
				<items>
					<item>Email</item>
					<item>Phone</item>
				</items>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Username</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<!--
				NOTE: type is STRING, not a masked type. Same reasoning recorded
				in this project's IntelliFire driver - the reference drivers here
				only use STRING and LIST, and a masked property type has not been
				confirmed in DriverEditor. If autocomplete offers one, switch this
				and Verification Code to it.

				The password is only needed for the initial sign-in; token
				refreshes use the stored JWT. As of v104 the driver CLEARS this
				property automatically once sign-in succeeds, so the plaintext
				password does not linger in the project or in project backups.
			-->
			<property>
				<name>Password</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Verification Code</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Lock ID</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>House ID</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<!--
				Leave BLANK for normal use: the driver carries the known
				x-august-api-key values and rotates to the next one automatically
				if Yale revokes the current one (HTTP 403 "API key is not valid").
				Only fill this in if every known key has been revoked and you have
				sourced a current one; setting it pins the key and disables rotation.
			-->
			<property>
				<name>API Key</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Poll Interval (seconds)</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default>60</default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>History Items</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default>20</default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Log Mode</name>
				<type>LIST</type>
				<readonly>false</readonly>
				<default>Off</default>
				<items>
					<item>Off</item>
					<item>Print</item>
					<item>Log</item>
					<item>Print and Log</item>
				</items>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Auth State</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>true</readonly>
				<default>Not signed in</default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Token Expires</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>true</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Lock Name</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>true</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Firmware Version</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>true</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Battery Level</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>true</readonly>
				<default>Unknown</default>
			</property>
			<property>
				<name>Discovered Locks</name>
				<type>STRING</type>
				<readonly>true</readonly>
				<default></default>
			</property>
		</properties>
	</config>

